{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And it shall be, when you come into the land, etc.</b> We do not find the expression 'וִירִשְׁתָּהּ וְיָשַׁבְתָּ בָּהּ' (you shall possess it and dwell in it) anywhere except here and in the section of the king, because after settlement and possession Israel grows fat and kicks and they seek for themselves matters of rulership like all the nations. So, too, in the section of first fruits the intention was to humble the pride of their hearts, which after possession and settlement would move their hearts to say that the land is theirs and by their own sword they possessed it, and they would forget HaShem. This is what it says: 'And it shall be, when you come into the land which HaShem your Elohim gives you for an inheritance' — for in truth you are not coming into the land by way of inheritance, for a house and wealth are an inheritance of fathers, but from HaShem is the earth given to you as a gift, on condition that you render recompense to Him and keep all His commandments. Yet your heart would not think so, but rather 'וִירִשְׁתָּהּ' — you would hold it as an heir, as though it were an inheritance to you from your fathers; 'וְיָשַׁבְתָּ בָּהּ' — you would dwell in it as a permanent resident, not as it is said (Leviticus 25:23): 'וְהָאָרֶץ לֹא תִמָּכֵר לִצְמִיתֻת כִּי לִי הָאָרֶץ כִּי גֵרִים וְתוֹשָׁבִים אַתֶּם' and so forth. And all this will bring you to pride, so that you forget HaShem; therefore I command you the commandment of first fruits. And thus the Rambam explained, that the essential reason for this commandment is submission and servitude toward HaShem, blessed be He. And the proof for this is what is said: 'הִגַּדְתִּי הַיּוֹם לַה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ' — 'הִגַּדְתִּי' is in the past tense, yet he has not yet declared anything at all; rather, it means to say that by this walking and bringing, it is already as though I have declared, in the manner of a confession: 'כִּי בָאתִי אֶל הָאָרֶץ' and so forth — meaning to say, not by way of inheritance, but in this manner, that HaShem swore to our fathers to give it to us; and I testify upon myself through the bringing of the first fruits that from HaShem the earth came to me as a portion and a gift. And this is what it says: 'וְלָקַחְתָּ מֵרֵאשִׁית' — 'and you shall take of the first.' It does not say 'וְנָתַתָּ' (and you shall give), but only 'וְלָקַחְתָּ' (and you shall take), to tell you that from this first which you bring to the house of HaShem to give acknowledgement and glory to His name, blessed be He — from it you shall take all the fruit of the land, and you shall merit all the fruit of the land, only after you have already acknowledged that you bring the first of your land which HaShem your Elohim gives you. For this land shall not be called 'your land' except after the bringing of the first fruits; and through this bringing you shall take for yourself all the fruit of the land. And this bringing is the declaration spoken of in the verse 'הִגַּדְתִּי הַיּוֹם.'"], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 26, :notes {0 "The word תוֹשָׁב can mean either 'resident alien' or 'permanent resident'; rendered here as 'permanent resident' to preserve the Kli Yakar's contrast between dwelling as though the land is one's own versus the Leviticus citation where Israel is called גֵרִים וְתוֹשָׁבִים."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:14:00.652970192Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}